  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective frameworks on employment, protection and rights for migrant workers including returnee migrants are in operation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh remains one of the world&#x2019;s largest migrant-sending countries, with more than 1 million workers migrating abroad in 2023 and remittances contributing around 5&#x2013;6% of GDP. Despite the sector&#x2019;s economic importance, labour migration governance continues to face challenges, including high recruitment costs, weak enforcement, limited awareness of rights, and inadequate protection, particularly for women migrant workers. Most migrants remain low- or semi-skilled, limiting access to higher-value jobs and increasing vulnerability to exploitation. Reintegration systems also remain weak, with many returnees facing unemployment and financial insecurity. Social protection for migrant workers also remains fragmented. 
Bangladesh has initiated reforms to promote safer, rights-based, and more transparent migration. Recent measures include the Sub-Agent Rule to formalize recruitment intermediaries, digitalization of overseas employment services, and strengthened reintegration initiatives. These efforts are complemented by initiatives that link skills development and recognition with safe and regular migration pathways.
The ILO&#x2019;s labour migration strategy for Bangladesh focuses on fair recruitment, rights-based governance, skills recognition, sustainable reintegration, and inclusive social protection in line with international labour standards. ILO technical assistance involves supporting key policy reforms led by the Ministry of Expatriates&#x2019; Welfare and Overseas Employment and their implementation. These include adoption of a National Action Plan for Reintegration, revision and adoption of the Expatriates&#x2019; Welfare and Overseas Employment Policy, and amendment of the Wage Earners&#x2019; Welfare Board Act 2018, establishment of a functional skilled mobility pathway between Bangladesh and EU Member States, and support in enhancing the operationalization of the Overseas Employment Platform and monitoring mechanism for licensed recruitment agencies.</narrative>
  </description>
